<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- A menu bar item, not an application to switch to. No Dock icon, and no
       window until asked for. -->
  <key>LSUIElement</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- The sentence macOS shows when it asks. Without this key it does not ask;
       it refuses, and the refusal looks like a bug in the microphone. -->
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>E.V. listens when you ask it to, and only then. Audio stays on this Mac.</string>

  <!-- E.V. runs its own core, and the core is Python in this repository. If
       the repository sits in Desktop, Documents or Downloads, macOS gates
       reading it: without these keys the app does not get asked, it gets
       EPERM, and the daemon dies on `pyvenv.cfg` before it prints anything
       (ADR 0031). A terminal already has this access, which is why it never
       came up until the app started the daemon itself. -->
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>E.V.'s own core lives in its project folder. It reads nothing else there.</string>
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>E.V.'s own core lives in its project folder. It reads nothing else there.</string>
  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>E.V.'s own core lives in its project folder. It reads nothing else there.</string>

  <!-- The camera, for one frame at a time. Without this key macOS does not
       ask; it refuses, and the refusal looks like a broken camera. -->
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>E.V. shows the camera on its own display while you have it open, and takes a picture when you ask a question about what it sees. Nothing is recorded and no frame is saved.</string>

  <!-- Music, Notes, Finder and the rest are reached by Apple Events. Until
       this is granted, tools that need it are denied before macOS is asked. -->
  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>E.V. controls apps you name — playing an album, opening a note — and nothing else.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
